										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>This content is copyright to <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.artemis.bm</a> and should not appear anywhere else, or an infringement has occurred.</p>
<p>Marsh clients are exploring greater use of third-party capital solutions, including sidecars for liability portfolios, as abundant reinsurance capacity and relatively benign catastrophe losses keep market conditions competitive ahead of the January 1 renewals, according to John Doyle, the firm’s President and Chief Executive Officer.<br />
<span id="more-151086"></span><br />
<img decoding="async" data-attachment-id="151090" data-permalink="https://www.artemis.bm/news/marsh-ceo-flags-client-interest-in-casualty-sidecars-notes-reinsurance-market-softening/attachment/john-doyle-marsh-ceo/" data-orig-file="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/john-doyle-marsh-ceo.jpg" data-orig-size="720,440" data-comments-opened="0" data-image-title="john-doyle-marsh-ceo" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-large-file="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/john-doyle-marsh-ceo.jpg" class="alignright wp-image-151090" src="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/john-doyle-marsh-ceo.jpg" alt="john-doyle-marsh-ceo" width="360" height="220" srcset="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/john-doyle-marsh-ceo.jpg 720w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/john-doyle-marsh-ceo-300x183.jpg 300w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/john-doyle-marsh-ceo-200x122.jpg 200w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/john-doyle-marsh-ceo-350x214.jpg 350w" sizes="(max-width: 360px) 100vw, 360px" />Speaking on Marsh’s Q3 2026 earnings call today, Doyle said short-tail reinsurance lines remained soft, driven by “abundant capacity from both traditional reinsurers and alternative capital.”</p>
<p>Doyle commented, &#8220;Our clients continue to explore alternative options to complement traditional strategies, including greater use of third-party capital solutions such as sidecar vehicles for their liability portfolios.&#8221;</p>
<p>Casualty reinsurance insurance-linked securities structures are a flavour of the day, of course, with numerous carriers now exploring how vehicles such as sidecars can help them to share risks and optimise capital.</p>
<p>In property catastrophe reinsurance, he pointed to relatively benign losses this year and growing capital as factors adding to competition.</p>
<p>“We expect these conditions to persist through the January 1 renewal cycle,” Doyle added.</p>
<p>Marsh Re’s Q3 2026 revenue fell 6% to $374 million, from $398 million a year earlier, on both a GAAP and underlying basis.</p>
<p>Asked how the reinsurance business will fare given current headwinds, Doyle said, “We’re a bit overweight in property catastrophe relative to some others, and we’re a bit overweight in the U.S. as well.</p>
<p>“It was a noisy third quarter, which is seasonally a smaller quarter, with some non-recurring items.</p>
<p>“We’ve also been affected to some extent by the consolidation that’s taken place in the industry during the course of the year.</p>
<p>“But, again, no excuses. We’re well positioned, and we believe we have the best reinsurance team in the market. We feel we’re well positioned.</p>
<p>“The team sees good growth opportunities in facultative reinsurance, M&amp;A advisory, alternative capital and alternative structures.</p>
<p>“We’re facing some market headwinds. We’ve seen them before, but we feel good about how we’re positioned over the medium to long term.”</p>
<p><a href="https://www.artemis.bm/news/marsh-ceo-flags-client-interest-in-casualty-sidecars-notes-reinsurance-market-softening/">Marsh CEO flags client interest in casualty sidecars, notes reinsurance market softening</a> was published by: <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.Artemis.bm</a><br />
Our <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/deal-directory/">catastrophe bond deal directory</a><br />
Sign up for <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/e-newsletter/">our free weekly email newsletter here</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
