										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>This content is copyright to <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.artemis.bm</a> and should not appear anywhere else, or an infringement has occurred.</p>
<p>Speaking yesterday at the ILS Bermuda Convergence 2026 conference, Bob Smith, President &amp; Co-CIO of fixed income and alternative solutions manager Sage Advisory, explained that he sees insurance-linked securities as an asset class that can expand the horizon of impact investing.<br />
<span id="more-151021"></span><br />
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-attachment-id="139674" data-permalink="https://www.artemis.bm/news/cat-bonds-ils-increasingly-compelling-as-essential-diversifiers-sage-advisory/attachment/sage-advisory-logo/" data-orig-file="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Sage-Advisory-Logo.jpg" data-orig-size="562,289" data-comments-opened="0" data-image-title="Sage-Advisory-Logo" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-large-file="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Sage-Advisory-Logo.jpg" class="alignright wp-image-139674" src="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Sage-Advisory-Logo.jpg" alt="Sage-Advisory-Logo" width="350" height="180" srcset="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Sage-Advisory-Logo.jpg 562w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Sage-Advisory-Logo-300x154.jpg 300w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Sage-Advisory-Logo-200x103.jpg 200w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Sage-Advisory-Logo-350x180.jpg 350w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 350px) 100vw, 350px" />Smith is a strong proponent for investing into catastrophe bonds and other natural catastrophe linked insurance-linked securities, seeing them as particularly valuable diversifiers that over the long-term have also outperformed many other fixed income asset classes.</p>
<p>But, as well as the low-correlation and differentiated return features that cat bonds and ILS can bring to investors portfolios, Smith also believes strongly in the sustainable investment qualities of the asset class.</p>
<p>&#8220;In this environment, one of the things that we always look for, because we are in many ways a sustainably focused investor as well at our firm, and we believe in responsible investing. So we&#8217;re always looking for investments that have impact and if there&#8217;s anything I can tell you at this conference, is indeed cat ILS is an impact investment,&#8221; Smith explained.</p>
<p>&#8220;It has economic purpose. There&#8217;s a lot of stuff in the asset backed world where I&#8217;m not really sure I get the economic purpose, other than what the management wants. Is it really good for the broader economy and society?&#8221;</p>
<p>Smith went on to say, &#8220;When we look at investments, we want to also be socially engaged. I think that ILS, quite frankly, needs to be front and centre in most impact portfolios.</p>
<p>&#8220;It&#8217;s an area that&#8217;s completely missed in terms of investors, and the problem with most impact investing is that it&#8217;s all about mitigation. But when we talk about this area, cat ILS, we&#8217;re talking about investing in resiliency, and resiliency is the number one byproduct of the effort in ILS.</p>
<p>&#8220;Yes, there is economic purpose, but we&#8217;re really about adaptation not mitigation. We&#8217;re about economic stability, and lastly, social risk. We want to make sure society continues on, and that there is not unrest.</p>
<p>&#8220;So, through the mechanism and the risk transfer mechanism offered by ILS, I think you expand the horizon for impact investing, and I think that is where the focus needs to be, because there&#8217;s a lot of money out there looking for this product, looking for this area of the market to invest in.&#8221;</p>
<p>You can read more of the firm&#8217;s thoughts on the impact topic in this article: <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/news/ils-cat-bonds-show-impact-investing-can-move-beyond-mitigation-into-resilience-sage-advisory/">ILS &amp; cat bonds show impact investing can move beyond mitigation into resilience: Sage Advisory</a>.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.artemis.bm/news/insurance-linked-securities-expand-the-horizon-for-impact-investing-smith-sage-advisory/">Insurance-linked securities expand the horizon for impact investing: Smith, Sage Advisory</a> was published by: <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.Artemis.bm</a><br />
Our <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/deal-directory/">catastrophe bond deal directory</a><br />
Sign up for <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/e-newsletter/">our free weekly email newsletter here</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
		<post-id xmlns="com-wordpress:feed-additions:1">151021</post-id>	</item>
		<item>
		<title>ILS has an upper-edge, strong narrative when it comes to proving diversification: Convergence</title>
		<link>https://www.artemis.bm/news/ils-has-an-upper-edge-strong-narrative-when-it-comes-to-proving-diversification-convergence/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=ils-has-an-upper-edge-strong-narrative-when-it-comes-to-proving-diversification-convergence</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Steve Evans]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 16:08:16 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[Insurance Linked Investments]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Cat bond]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Catastrophe bond]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Insurance linked securities]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Insurance-linked investments]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Reinsurance linked investment]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.artemis.bm/?p=151016</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>This content is copyright to <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.artemis.bm</a> and should not appear anywhere else, or an infringement has occurred.</p>
<p>For investors in the insurance-linked securities asset class, when pitching a potential allocation to their investment committees the task is helped by the fact ILS has a strong narrative to tell, when it comes to proving its diversification benefits and has the data to support that, speakers at the Convergence 2026 conference in Bermuda said [&#8230;]</p>
<p><a href="https://www.artemis.bm/news/ils-has-an-upper-edge-strong-narrative-when-it-comes-to-proving-diversification-convergence/">ILS has an upper-edge, strong narrative when it comes to proving diversification: Convergence</a> was published by: <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.Artemis.bm</a><br />
Our <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/deal-directory/">catastrophe bond deal directory</a><br />
Sign up for <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/e-newsletter/">our free weekly email newsletter here</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>This content is copyright to <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.artemis.bm</a> and should not appear anywhere else, or an infringement has occurred.</p>
<p>For investors in the insurance-linked securities asset class, when pitching a potential allocation to their investment committees the task is helped by the fact ILS has a strong narrative to tell, when it comes to proving its diversification benefits and has the data to support that, speakers at the Convergence 2026 conference in Bermuda said today.<br />
<span id="more-151016"></span><br />
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-attachment-id="107520" data-permalink="https://www.artemis.bm/news/many-ways-to-generate-ils-alpha-but-managing-expectations-key-convergence-panel/attachment/ils-bermuda-2023-image4/" data-orig-file="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/ils-bermuda-2023-image4.jpg" data-orig-size="822,484" data-comments-opened="0" data-image-title="ils-bermuda-2023-image4" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-large-file="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/ils-bermuda-2023-image4.jpg" class="alignright wp-image-107520" src="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/ils-bermuda-2023-image4.jpg" alt="ils-bermuda-2023-image4" width="360" height="212" srcset="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/ils-bermuda-2023-image4.jpg 822w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/ils-bermuda-2023-image4-300x177.jpg 300w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/ils-bermuda-2023-image4-768x452.jpg 768w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/ils-bermuda-2023-image4-200x118.jpg 200w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/ils-bermuda-2023-image4-350x206.jpg 350w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 360px) 100vw, 360px" />During a panel discussion at the event featuring end-investor allocators and an investment firm that provides specialist advisory and portfolio construction services, the speakers explained that while it&#8217;s not always the easiest asset class to present to an investment committee, the fact ILS has the data to evidence the diversification story is helpful.</p>
<p>Joe Tolen, Senior Investment Director at specialist advisor Cambridge Associates, said that explaining the true diversification benefits of some private and alternative asset classes can be a challenge, but when it comes to insurance-linked securities there is an easier story to tell.</p>
<p>&#8220;I think that&#8217;s a super-important point and something people in this room should try to build on, is that proof of diversification. Because I&#8217;ve struggled with it in my uncorrelated credit branding of trying to show certain pockets of litigation finance, and you can anecdotally tell someone that the returns of a law firm lending strategy is based on a diversified pool of fee receivables that have nothing to do with central bank policy or corporate earnings GDP growth. You can tell them that, but because all the strategies are private, it&#8217;s very difficult to show them that,&#8221; Tolen explained.</p>
<p>He added, &#8220;So I think ILS really has an upper-edge, in having the data available and being able to prove that diversification.</p>
<p>&#8220;With ILS specifically, I think diversification is the major selling point. It&#8217;s what gets people to pay attention, and it&#8217;s what gets them at the table.&#8221;</p>
<p>Scott Radke, CEO &amp; Co-CIO, New Holland Capital, an allocator to the insurance-linked securities asset class, commented, &#8220;I think it starts with a governance situation. So trustees or a board and CIO that&#8217;s open to innovation, or at least not opposed to it, so there&#8217;s a climate which is receptive to these kinds of somewhat unorthodox ideas.</p>
<p>&#8220;Then it&#8217;s an organisation which is focused on portfolio construction, so they think about it through that lens, and as a result, can appreciate the value of the diversification that something like ILS offers. &#8221;</p>
<p>Nidhi Sharma, Director &amp; Senior Portfolio Manager – Alpha Alternatives, PSP Investments (another allocator), further explained, &#8220;I actually think ILS has a very strong narrative as an asset class. The lack of correlation is, I think, probably the most intuitive, strongest to prove out. It has a decent history of data and indices that you could check that against. The liquidity is actually really attractive, it&#8217;s short duration, it fits in the portfolio. There&#8217;s enough scale, it&#8217;s a market that has continued to grow and evolve, and you have different flavours of executing in this for different structures.</p>
<p>&#8220;I actually think that if you&#8217;re a long-term investor who will accept that there will be drawdowns and losses in certain years, which is the case with every single asset class, and that you can weather that kind of volatility, and that&#8217;s a portfolio construction question, then I think it&#8217;s easy to make the case.&#8221;</p>
<p>The panel also discussed the fact that, for investment professionals presenting the asset class to their boards there can be an element of career risk, in championing an investment strategy that continues to be considered novel at many investment committees.</p>
<p>Something that the availability of data to provide examples of the potential upside and downside can also assist with.</p>
<p>Radke commented, &#8220;To get to the finish line, you need a champion, and the champion isn&#8217;t the CIO. The champion&#8217;s the mid-level person who decides to make a run at it, probably you know in some ways unwisely at least initially, where they&#8217;re taking a little bit of career risk.&#8221;</p>
<p>Sharma added, &#8220;My impression is that ILS is easy to like, it&#8217;s hard to understand the risk. You have a lot of models that will tell you what happens in the worst 1% of outcomes, the worst 10% of outcomes. But, I think ultimately, sitting in an IC, looking at the people around and telling them that if you have Hurricane Katrina today, and if it goes through Miami, Philadelphia, and New York, you are looking to lose pretty much all of your capital, and that is the worst-case scenario. Are you comfortable with that? That&#8217;s where the question of enthusiasm, career risk actually comes in and I think having that candid conversation and telling people that this is what you&#8217;re finally signing up to, that in this scenario you can lose this much money and do you have that actual willingness to lose, then of course I think the pathway is clear. And if not, then either it&#8217;s a no, or scale back, or right size.&#8221;</p>
<p>Tolen further stated, &#8220;I think you both bringing up career risk is super important, but it depends on the investment knowledge of the IC as well, especially at a lot of public pension plans. For instance, that IC, that board they might not be made up of investment professionals, which can make it a little bit scary from a behavioural economic standpoint to back an industry that has the word catastrophe in one of their products.</p>
<p>&#8220;I think it&#8217;s a lot easier for them to just think, oh, you know what I would rather go down the more conventional route and potentially leave something on the table than enter into something where it could be another Katrina and it could be six months after you invest, and all of a sudden you have egg on your face, and you&#8217;re getting questions about why did you allocate it to this space. So it can be really challenging from from a career standpoint.&#8221;</p>
<p>Sharma added, &#8220;I can second that by saying, it&#8217;s a lot easier to present a music catalogue that everybody&#8217;s heard and can find their favourites in, versus something that is a cat bond.&#8221;</p>
<p><a href="https://www.artemis.bm/news/ils-has-an-upper-edge-strong-narrative-when-it-comes-to-proving-diversification-convergence/">ILS has an upper-edge, strong narrative when it comes to proving diversification: Convergence</a> was published by: <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.Artemis.bm</a><br />
Our <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/deal-directory/">catastrophe bond deal directory</a><br />
Sign up for <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/e-newsletter/">our free weekly email newsletter here</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
		<post-id xmlns="com-wordpress:feed-additions:1">151016</post-id>	</item>
		<item>
		<title>Cat bonds offer flexibility across fixed income and alternative investment allocations: Victory Capital</title>
		<link>https://www.artemis.bm/news/cat-bonds-offer-flexibility-across-fixed-income-and-alternative-investment-allocations-victory-capital/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=cat-bonds-offer-flexibility-across-fixed-income-and-alternative-investment-allocations-victory-capital</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Jack Willard]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Wed, 14 Oct 2026 13:00:42 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[Catastrophe Bonds]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Cat bond]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Catastrophe bond]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Insurance linked securities]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Insurance-linked investments]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[reinsurance]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.artemis.bm/?p=150628</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>This content is copyright to <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.artemis.bm</a> and should not appear anywhere else, or an infringement has occurred.</p>
<p>Given that catastrophe bonds are known for having a low correlation to traditional financial markets, global asset management firm Victory Capital has highlighted how the asset class&#8217;s portfolio implementation flexibility makes it viable for either an investor’s fixed income allocation or its alternative investment strategy. In a recent article, Victory Capital outlines that many investors [&#8230;]</p>
<p><a href="https://www.artemis.bm/news/cat-bonds-offer-flexibility-across-fixed-income-and-alternative-investment-allocations-victory-capital/">Cat bonds offer flexibility across fixed income and alternative investment allocations: Victory Capital</a> was published by: <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.Artemis.bm</a><br />
Our <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/deal-directory/">catastrophe bond deal directory</a><br />
Sign up for <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/e-newsletter/">our free weekly email newsletter here</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>This content is copyright to <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.artemis.bm</a> and should not appear anywhere else, or an infringement has occurred.</p>
<p>Given that catastrophe bonds are known for having a low correlation to traditional financial markets, global asset management firm Victory Capital has highlighted how the asset class&#8217;s portfolio implementation flexibility makes it viable for either an investor’s fixed income allocation or its alternative investment strategy.<br />
<span id="more-150628"></span><br />
<img loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-attachment-id="150647" data-permalink="https://www.artemis.bm/news/cat-bonds-offer-flexibility-across-fixed-income-and-alternative-investment-allocations-victory-capital/attachment/victory-capital-logo-2/" data-orig-file="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Victory-Capital-Logo.png" data-orig-size="603,309" data-comments-opened="0" data-image-title="Victory-Capital-Logo" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-large-file="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Victory-Capital-Logo.png" class="alignright wp-image-150647" src="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Victory-Capital-Logo.png" alt="" width="370" height="190" srcset="https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Victory-Capital-Logo.png 603w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Victory-Capital-Logo-300x154.png 300w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Victory-Capital-Logo-200x102.png 200w, https://www.artemis.bm/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Victory-Capital-Logo-350x179.png 350w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 370px) 100vw, 370px" />In a recent article, Victory Capital outlines that many investors prioritise the incorporation of strategies with returns that either have low or no correlation to other major asset classes, along with securing new sources of income that have the potential to generate attractive risk-adjusted returns regardless of what’s happening across the broader financial market.</p>
<p>This is where the use of catastrophe bonds, a core component of the insurance-linked securities (ILS) market can play a crucial role.</p>
<p>As Artemis’ readers are aware, the cat bond market has gained heavy momentum in recent years, as organisations have become more familiar with the asset class, which in turn has led to a record influx of new sponsors venturing into the space.</p>
<p>While catastrophe bonds carry event-driven principal risk, Victory Capital importantly highlights that history has shown that investors who deploy cat bonds as a strategic allocation, have not suffered sustained principal losses.</p>
<p>“The insurance industry has demonstrated the ability to reprice risk efficiently (i.e., raise premiums), particularly after a catastrophic event. Thus, we think that the true risk profile of cat bonds is one of the misunderstood aspects of this fixed income asset class,&#8221; Victory Capital explained.</p>
<p>Further backing this claim, Victory Capital highlights that the Swiss Re Global Cat Bond Index has managed to deliver a positive performance throughout 18 of the past 19 years.</p>
<p>The firm also underscored how catastrophe bonds as represented by the Swiss Re Global Cat Bond Index, have offered more attractive risk/return characteristics than many more well-known asset classes have throughout the past decade.</p>
<p>“Using the return history of Swiss Re Global Cat Bond Index (2007-2025) as a proxy for the asset class, we evaluated its three-year annualized rolling returns since inception. The range of outcomes were from 2.58% to 16.07%. In 100% (17 of 17) of the rolling three-year periods, the index achieved absolute positive returns, and in all rolling three periods, the returns were in excess of 2%. Of course, past performance does not guarantee future results,” Victory Capital added.</p>
<p>The asset management firm also notes that while financial markets are heavily focused on areas such as inflation data, corporate earnings, tariffs, geopolitics and gross domestic product projections, cat bond investors are much less concerned by these economic metrics, given that they are largely uninfluenced by such factors, and therefore have a low correlation compared to other broad asset classes.</p>
<p>However, the firm warns that much like any investment, there are risks that investors must consider when venturing into the cat bond market.</p>
<p>“A cat bond investment could suffer principal losses upon the occurrence of one or more natural disasters that trigger losses of a certain magnitude. Thus, cat bonds may not be suitable for every investor’s portfolio,&#8221; Victory Capital said.</p>
<p>&#8220;However, an actively managed cat bond portfolio is designed to balance geographic exposures, while also exploiting possible structural inefficiencies in the market,” the firm added.</p>
<p>Concluding: “In the end, we believe that a cat bond allocation may be well positioned to help solve for several investor objectives. The sources of risk, return, and liquidity are unlike anything in a traditional diversified investment portfolio, and the portfolio implementation flexibility could make them viable for either an investor’s fixed income allocation or an alternative investment bucket.”</p>
<p><a href="https://www.artemis.bm/news/cat-bonds-offer-flexibility-across-fixed-income-and-alternative-investment-allocations-victory-capital/">Cat bonds offer flexibility across fixed income and alternative investment allocations: Victory Capital</a> was published by: <a href="https://www.artemis.bm">www.Artemis.bm</a><br />
Our <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/deal-directory/">catastrophe bond deal directory</a><br />
Sign up for <a href="https://www.artemis.bm/e-newsletter/">our free weekly email newsletter here</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
		<post-id xmlns="com-wordpress:feed-additions:1">150628</post-id>	</item>
	</channel>
</rss>